{"decision": "repair", "edits": [{"start_line": 241, "end_line": 248, "replacement": "            if (byte_accepted)
            begin
                r_byte <= i_byte[7:0];
                r_state <= LLSDSPI_START;
                r_idle <= 1'b0;
                o_mosi <= i_byte[7];
                o_sclk <= 1'b1;
            end else"}, {"start_line": 250, "end_line": 268, "replacement": "        end else if (o_sclk && r_z_counter)
        begin
            o_mosi <= r_byte[7];
            r_byte <= { r_byte[6:0], 1'b1 };
            r_state <= r_state + 1;
            o_sclk <= 1'b0;
            if (r_state >= LLSDSPI_START+8)
            begin
                r_state <= LLSDSPI_HOTIDLE;
                r_idle <= 1'b1;
                o_stb <= 1'b1;
                o_byte <= r_ireg;
                o_sclk <= 1'b1;
            end else
                r_state <= r_state + 1;
        end else if (r_z_counter) begin
            r_ireg <= { r_ireg[6:0], i_miso };
            o_sclk <= 1'b1;
        end"}], "explanation": "Discovery traces confirm the SPI clock (o_sck/o_sclk) toggles at system-clock rate instead of the configured divider rate. The first mismatch at cycle 101 shows o_sck=1 when 0 is expected, and subsequent cycles show it stuck high. The root cause is the bit-transmission loop (lines 250-268) toggling SCK and advancing state every cycle without checking r_z_counter. The fix gates both the SCK-falling and SCK-rising edges with r_z_counter so the divider controls SPI frequency. Additionally, the HOTIDLE state (lines 241-248) forced the last transmitted bit to 1 and advanced state by one extra step, corrupting back-to-back byte transmission; the fix makes HOTIDLE identical to IDLE for correct serial bit order. Both edits preserve the public interface, startup timing, and Wishbone-qualified acknowledgments."}
